{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794443116,"nonce":"OEt/2ef0m2aZNM2UfU3GbCQ+jRg=","title":"Rotating a motion container turns its box but not its label, and never its shadow","message":"Found by the animation-path harness (crates/blinc_recorder/tests/animation_paths.rs; BLINC_ANIM_PATHS_OUT=\u003cdir\u003e writes a contact sheet for each scene). A bordered, shadowed card with a text label sits in a motion container whose parent is the card's size, so the pivot is the card's centre.\n\n- Only a rotate binding (scene \"motion rotate\"): the box turns about its centre, the label stays level at the rest position, and the shadow stays an axis-aligned rounded rectangle at the rest position, left behind the turning box.\n- Translate, scale, rotate and fade bound together (\"motion all\"): the same. The label stays level and drifts off the box's centre as it turns; the shadow stays behind.\n- Rotate and scale in an inner container under a translating outer one (\"motion nested\"): the label turns with the card. The shadow still does not.\n- A CSS keyframe or transition rotation turns the label with the card.\n\nThe frames are identical in a full paint and on the patched path a window uses, so this is how the renderer draws it, not a patching error. In CSS a rotation turns the element's whole content, shadow included.\n\nNot investigated: why the label follows in the nested scene and not in the others (the scene with scale bound seems to differ from rotate alone only by the nested layering, not by the scale), and whether the shadow's offset is meant to stay in screen space. The GPU node table (122c606) has to decide what rotation does to glyphs and shadows, so this wants settling before it goes live.","files":null}]}